  top_ideas: {'top_ideas': ['C1S1 — Sensors × Extreme Cold: Passive thermal enclosure using phase-change materials protects sensors without active power; Impact: 9', 'C3S3 — Staff × Small Volumes: Rural pharmacies aggregate orders into pooled shipments to amortize high unit delivery costs; Impact: 9', 'C2S2 — Containers × Sparse Roads: Hardened village hub lockers consolidate deliveries to reach isolated settlements during road closures; Impact: 8', 'C5S4 — Compliance × Strict Logging: Ruggedized IoT tags record temperature locally and sync only upon handover to eliminate manual entry; Impact: 8', 'C4S5 — Drones × Long Distances: Shared municipal drone corridors amortize infrastructure costs across multiple healthcare facilities; Impact: 7'], 'synthesis': 'The matrix reveals a tension between high-tech autonomy and low-tech resilience; the most viable innovations leverage local human networks to overcome economic and infrastructural deficits rather than relying solely on advanced hardware. Physical preservation innovations consistently score higher than communication improvements because they address the existential failure mode: cold-induced battery and storage collapse. Ultimately, the solution space favors hybrid models that share costs across pharmacies and infrastructure across regions to offset sparse density and small volume constraints.'}

